# GATE-(c) PROPOSAL — RE-ARM ONLY: lift the halt, unpause the queue, end live-but-idle (repo-only, no-effect)

**Status: PROPOSAL ONLY — repo-only. This package makes no
production call, mutates nothing, and authorizes nothing.** It
exists so Rowan can review the re-arm plan and Julie can later
decide, separately, whether to execute it.

**Naming (Rowan's, followed exactly):** Gate (c) is **RE-ARM
ONLY** — the halt flip + the queue unpause, ending with the
system live but idle. **Generation — the rest of Forever
Pucked — is a LATER, distinct gate** (its own fresh quote, its
own decision). **Chapter 3 generation is likewise a distinct
approval of its own, independent of the generation decision —
neither requires the other.** Neither is requested, scoped, or priced by
this proposal beyond stating their separateness (§10).

**Direction:** Rowan's gate-(b) closure ruling (issue #15,
comment 6547328443): *"Wren may prepare a bounded Gate-(c)
re-arm proposal with fresh full reconcile ok true at
execution, halt/queue sequencing, stop and containment gates,
and distinct Chapter 3 generation approval."* Every element
she named is carried: the fresh-census requirement is §4, the
sequencing is §6 (unpause LAST), the stop/containment gates
are §§3, 6, 7, and Chapter 3's distinctness is §§1, 8, 10.

**Precedent:** Gate-7's re-arm (proposal + A0–A6 execution,
2026-10-31, ALL PASS) is this proposal's template — same two
mutations, same order, same verify-only settings step, same
armed-idle verification shape. **Where this proposal differs
from Gate-7, it says so explicitly:**

1. **Different build.** Gate-7 re-armed build `6a73d962…`. The
   live build is now `945875bc…` (the combined remediation +
   quota + operator-routes build, deployed at gate (a)). The
   stale-work assessment in §5 is traced **fresh** in this
   build's deployed source — Gate-7's conclusions are not
   copied.
2. **Different plan inventory.** Gate-7 faced two plans
   (retired + complete). There are now **three**: the smoke
   plan (complete), the old Chapter 2 plan (retired), and the
   fresh Chapter 2 plan (complete, Gate-8). A third
   `schedule_dead` row exists (the fresh plan's grant lease).
   §5 assesses this inventory, not Gate-7's.
3. **The quota guard is now ARMED** (gate (b), 2026-10-31).
   Gate-7's build had no armed counter. §4 adds the
   fresh-census / `ok === true` precondition Rowan directed,
   and §5 notes where the armed guard sits relative to the
   stale-work fences (behind them — a backstop, never the
   proof).
4. **The flip mechanism's parts manifest** is the gate-(a)
   operative envelope (28 parts), not the disposition
   envelope Gate-7 used (§6, R3).

Labels: **CONFIRMED** = read back from production or verified
in the cited record. **INFERRED** = deduced from cited
recorded evidence, basis stated. **OPEN** = unresolved,
carried as an explicit verification step or STOP condition —
never silently assumed.

---

## 0. Julie-facing summary (plain language)

Gate (b) finished the bookkeeping: the request counter is on
and reconciles perfectly. The system is still parked — the
generation switch is off and the work queue is paused.

This proposal is **only** about un-parking it: flip the switch
back on, then — last — unpause the queue. Nothing would be
generated by this gate. No book work is submitted, no provider
is called, nothing is spent. The system would end **live but
idle**: able to generate, with nothing asked of it.

Before the flip, the executor re-checks everything (the
pre-state in §2 must match exactly, and a **brand-new full
reconciliation must read `ok: true` at that moment** — the
clean result from gate (b) is not reused). After the flip,
the system is watched through two long check windows (at
least 30 minutes each) so that any of the leftover historical
work (the retired plan's stale job, the finished plans, the
parked Chapter 3 job) stirring on its own would be seen. If
anything at all moves, the gate's standing order is to
re-park immediately — flipping back is always allowed inside
this gate, no new permission needed, because returning to the
parked state is the safe direction.

Generating the rest of Forever Pucked stays a separate later
decision for you, with a fresh price quote first. Chapter 3
stays parked and is a separate decision of its own — the two
decisions don't depend on each other, and you can take them
in either order. (You listen in book order, so you'd probably
choose the rest of the book first — but that's your listening
preference, not a rule of the system.)

---

## 1. Scope — exactly two mutations, in this order

When — and only when — Julie separately approves execution of
this gate, it performs exactly:

- **R-flip:** `GENERATION_HALT` `"1"` → `"0"`, by the
  metadata-only mechanism (§6, R3): the freshly downloaded
  live script parts are re-uploaded **byte-identical** with
  the upload metadata's parsed diff being **exactly one
  value** (the halt binding). The script etag is therefore
  expected **UNCHANGED** (`d63b61c3…` — the etag is
  content-derived over the module parts, and no part
  changes). Observability is re-read after the upload; if
  it has regressed (a code upload can silently reset it —
  standing lesson), the conditional settings PATCH applies:
  multipart PATCH to `/workers/scripts/{name}/settings`
  (single `settings` part, observability object only), then
  verification that the script etag is still unchanged.
  (At every deploy in this chain to date, observability has
  in fact survived and no PATCH was needed; the rule is
  carried anyway.)
- **R-unpause, LAST:** the queue `opalreader-generation-v2`
  is unpaused via cf-api PUT (`queue_name` +
  `settings.delivery_paused: false`), with an independent
  read-back. No other step follows it except verification.

**End state:** worker live (`generation: "enabled"`), queue
delivering, quota guard armed, **no plan submitted by this
gate, nothing queued by this gate, nothing generated,
nothing spent**. **Batch stays unarmed:** the Standard-only
arming state is a property of the deployed code
(`d6StandardOnly: true` is stamped by the production entry
itself, not a binding), so the metadata flip cannot change
it — CONFIRMED in source (§5, note).

**Non-goals:** everything in §8's NOT-authorized list. In
particular: no `SubmitJob`, no intent, no generation, no
provider call, no Chapter 3 action of any kind.

